# WHAT THIS REPOSITORY DEPLOYS AS. STATED, not defaulted, and the difference is the whole line.
#
# The default is the REPOSITORY's name — `qits-landing-app` — and the `-app` suffix is a statement
# about how this repository is BUILT, not about what runs. Defaulted, that role word would end up in
# the swarm service, the wire alias (`dev-qits-landing-app`), the derived `host` label
# (`landing-app`), the image path `qits/qits-landing-app`, the provisioned database name and the
# `QITS_APPLICATION` the container boots with — six places where the deployed identity would carry a
# build detail forever, because CHANGING this value later is a decommission and a new application
# rather than a rename.
#
# `qits-landing` is therefore what the image in `.config/qits/release.yml` is called
# (`qits/qits-landing`), and the two are one decision: the deployer pulls
# `qits/<application>:<version>` and the release step pushes the coordinate this file names.
application: qits-landing

# THE PATH PREFIX THE EDGE ROUTES HERE ON EVERY VHOST.
#
# `/landing`, and the reasoning is worth writing down because a landing page is the one application
# on the estate with no machine surface to justify a route.
#
# 1. A route is NOT OPTIONAL here. `host:` below is refused by DeploymentSpecParser without at least
#    one `routes:` entry — "a host with nothing behind it serves nobody" — so the question is which
#    prefix, never whether.
# 2. `/` IS SYNTACTICALLY LEGAL AND WOULD BE A DISASTER. The routes regex admits the bare root, and
#    a route is path-routed on EVERY vhost — so `routes: /` would claim every path of every
#    application on the platform for this one. It is the one value this file must not hold.
# 3. `/landing` IS THE APPLICATION'S OWN SEGMENT, the shape every sibling uses: qits-ci takes `/ci`,
#    qits-artifacts `/artifacts`, qits-configuration `/configuration` — the application name minus
#    its `qits-` prefix, which is also exactly what `host:` derives to.
# 4. AND IT RENDERS. `src/app/app.routes.ts` carries a `**` route onto the landing page precisely so
#    the prefix is not a 404: `<any-vhost>/landing` serves the same server-rendered page the root of
#    this application's own host does. A declared route that answered nothing would be a name that
#    leads nowhere, which is the thing rule 1 exists to prevent.
routes: /landing

# THE DNS LABEL THIS APPLICATION IS SERVED AT — `landing.<env>.<domain>` — and it is the
# load-bearing line of this file.
#
# It is the value the DERIVATION would have produced anyway (`qits-landing` minus the `qits-`
# prefix), and it is stated all the same, because a later epic reserves this label as the project's
# ROOT. A derived label is a consequence of a name; a stated one is a claim, and this file is where
# the claim has to be readable. It also survives a change to the derivation rule, which a default
# would not.
host: landing

# HEALTH_PATH, NOT HEALTH_CMD, and a path this server actually serves.
#
# The deployer runs `curl` INSIDE the container against this path and rolls the deployment back when
# it does not answer, so the value and the route in `src/server.ts` are one decision spelled twice.
# The DERIVED default is `/landing/q/health/ready` — a Quarkus shape, from a service that mounts
# smallrye-health under its non-application root. Nothing here serves it, and taking the default
# would fail every deployment as unhealthy after the container had come up perfectly well.
#
# `health_cmd:` is the alternative and is wrong for this image: it exists for a deployable with no
# HTTP surface at all (postgres, which has neither curl nor anything on 8080). A node server answers
# a path.
#
# It sits UNDER the published `/landing` prefix deliberately — the probe runs in the container
# either way, but a readiness path outside every published route would be answerable from inside the
# container and from nowhere else, which makes it untestable from a workstation.
health_path: /landing/health

# NO `upstream_port:`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN FORGOTTEN. DeploymentSpecParser's
# DEFAULT_UPSTREAM_PORT is 8080; `src/server.ts` defaults to 8080 and `docker/Dockerfile` states
# `ENV PORT=8080`. Three spellings of one number is already one too many, and a fourth that could
# drift from the other three buys nothing. The scaffold's own default is 4000 — that is what was
# changed, and this comment is why.

# NO `navigation-entries:` and no `navigation:`. This application is the front door itself, not a
# row in somebody else's sidebar: an entry would put "Landing" in the chrome of every application
# the shell draws navigation for.

# NO `update_order:`, so the default `start-first` stands — the successor comes up beside the
# predecessor and a failed health gate is a lossless rollback. The siblings that say `stop-first` do
# so because they bind a host port or hold a single-writer store, and this application does neither:
# it publishes no port of its own, writes nothing, and is reached through the edge on the overlay.

# NO `resources:` and no `volumes:`. The page is static text compiled into the bundle; there is no
# database to provision and nothing to keep between deployments.
